  {
   "heading": "Before a vowel",
   "body": "With verbs that start with a vowel or silent h, *je* becomes *j'*, and *nous, vous, ils, elles* link with a z sound: *j'habite*, *nous habitons* /nu.z‿a.bi.tɔ̃/, *ils aiment* /il.z‿ɛm/."
  },
  {
   "heading": "One English tense, one French tense",
   "body": "The French present covers both English \"I work\" and \"I'm working\": *Je travaille* means either. *Qu'est-ce que tu fais ? — Je regarde la télé.* (What are you doing? — I'm watching TV.)"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Everyday -er verbs",
   "body": "*parler* (speak), *habiter* (live), *travailler* (work), *aimer* (like, love), *regarder* (watch, look at), *écouter* (listen to), *manger* (eat), *étudier* (study), *jouer* (play), *chercher* (look for), *rentrer* (go home), *commencer* (begin).\n\nSome more forms, all following the same pattern: *j'aime*, *tu écoutes*, *il regarde*, *elle étudie*, *nous jouons*, *vous habitez*, *vous travaillez*, *ils regardent*, *elles cherchent*.\n\nTwo small spelling changes keep the sound the same: *nous mangeons* (an e keeps the g soft) and *nous commençons* (a ç keeps the c soft)."
  }
